Describe the process by which visual information is transmitted and processed within these neural layers.
Based on the provided context, here is the description of the process by which visual information is transmitted and processed within the neural layers:

Visual information is first perceived by the retina, where photoreceptors absorb light and transmit signals to bipolar cells. The bipolar cells then transmit signals to ganglion cells, which have axons that converge and diverge to form center-surround receptive fields. These signals are then transmitted laterally by horizontal and amacrine cells to other neurons in the retina. The processed information is then transmitted from the retina to the brain through the optic nerve, specifically to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) in the thalamus. From the LGN, the visual information is transmitted to the occipital lobe, where it is processed and recognized in the visual cortex. In the visual cortex, neurons are tuned to simple visual characteristics such as orientation, spatial frequency, size, color, and shape. The processed information is then stored in long-term memory, with layer 6 cells of the V2 cortex playing a crucial role in the storage of object recognition memory and the conversion of short-term object memories into long-term memories.
