How has the interpretation of the Second Amendment evolved over time, particularly in relation to gun control laws and court decisions?
The interpretation of the Second Amendment has evolved significantly over time, particularly in relation to gun control laws and court decisions. Prior to 2001, every circuit court decision endorsed the "collective right" model, which held that the Second Amendment only protected the right to bear arms in the context of a state militia. However, with the Fifth Circuit's opinion in "United States v. Emerson" in 2001, some circuit courts began to recognize that the Second Amendment protects an individual right to bear arms. This individual right interpretation was further solidified by the Supreme Court's decisions in "District of Columbia v. Heller" (2008) and "McDonald v. Chicago" (2010), which held that the Second Amendment protects an individual right to possess firearms and applies to the states.
