Discuss the impact of later scientists, such as Lagrange and Laplace, on the development of modern planetary motion theories.
Later scientists, such as Joseph Louis Lagrange and Pierre Simon Laplace, built upon the work of earlier astronomers like Tycho Brahe and Johannes Kepler. They made significant contributions to the development of modern planetary motion theories by refining the understanding of planetary orbits and stability.

Lagrange and Laplace worked on the three-body problem, which involves the gravitational interactions between three celestial bodies. Their work led to more accurate predictions about the motions of the Moon and planets, and allowed for the estimation of the masses of planets and moons from their perturbations.

Laplace, in particular, is known for his efforts to prove the stability of the Solar System. He and other mathematicians and astronomers, such as Lagrange, Gauss, and Poincaré, searched for evidence of stability in planetary motions, which led to many mathematical developments and successive "proofs" of stability for the Solar System.

Their work laid the foundation for later advances in astronomy, including the development of more accurate models of planetary motion and the understanding of the behavior of complex celestial systems.
