What is the New Testament in the Bible and what are its contents, specifically the order of its books, to identify the first book.
The New Testament in the Bible is a collection of 27 books of 4 different genres of Christian literature. The contents of the New Testament include:

1.  The Gospels (narrative literature, account and history of the Apostolic age)
2.  The Acts of the Apostles
3.  The Epistles (letters)
4.  The Apocalypse (apocalyptic literature, also called prophetic)

The order of the New Testament books varies across different traditions, but the mainstream consensus is that the New Testament was written in Greek. 

In the Literal English Version (LEV), the order of the New Testament books is as follows:

1.  The Gospels (Matthew, Mark, Luke, John)
2.  The Acts of the Apostles
3.  The General Epistles (directly after the Gospels and Acts)
4.  The Pauline Epistles (at the end, just before Revelation)
5.  The Apocalypse (Revelation)

The first book of the New Testament is the Gospel of Matthew.
