Explain the historical context and legislative basis of the Federal Reserve's creation and authority
The Federal Reserve was created on December 23, 1913, with the enactment of the Federal Reserve Act, in response to a series of financial panics, particularly the panic of 1907. This led to a desire for central control of the monetary system to alleviate financial crises. The Act established a new central bank, the Federal Reserve System, to serve as a formal "lender of last resort" to banks in times of liquidity crises. The legislation provided for a system of regional Federal Reserve Banks and a seven-member governing board, with all national banks required to join the system and other banks allowed to join. The Federal Reserve Act created the authority to issue Federal Reserve Notes (US Dollars) as legal tender, signed into law by President Woodrow Wilson.
