1.  The 26th Amendment (1971): Lowered the minimum voting age from 21 to 18.
2.  States lowering their minimum drinking age to 18 (1971-1973): In response to the 26th Amendment, many states lowered their minimum drinking age to 18.
3.  States raising their minimum drinking age to 19 or 20 (1976-1983): Some states voluntarily raised their minimum drinking age to 19 or 20 to combat drunk driving fatalities.
4.  National Minimum Drinking Age Act (1984): Congress passed this act, which required states to raise their minimum drinking age to 21 by October 1986 or lose 10% of their federal highway funding.
5.  Mothers Against Drunk Driving (MADD) pressure: MADD's efforts to raise awareness about drunk driving and pressure the federal government to increase the drinking age contributed to the eventual passage of the National Minimum Drinking Age Act.

